{"bill":{"id":"119-hr-8736","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"8736","title":"Restoration of Employment Choice for Adults with Disabilities Act","introducedDate":"2026-05-12","latestActionDate":"2026-05-21","latestActionText":"Ordered to be Reported (Amended) by the Yeas and Nays: 18 - 15.","policyArea":"Labor and Employment","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"G000576","cosponsorBioguideIds":["W000812","O000086","F000471","S001213","B001301"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Restoration of Employment Choice for Adults with Disabilities Act This bill relaxes requirements that allow entities with a special wage certificate to pay subminimum wages to individuals with disabilities. Specifically, the bill eliminates a requirement that an individual with a disability who is age 24 or younger receive pre-employment transition services and meet specified vocational rehabilitation criteria before beginning subminimum wage work. The bill also relaxes career counseling and service referral requirements that apply to an individual with a disability who is paid at a subminimum wage. Current law requires an entity to ensure that an individual employed at subminimum wage receives periodic career counseling, information, and referrals to other services from the designated state agency. Under the bill, this requirement is met if the employer makes a documented effort to contact the state agency for those resources on behalf of the employee, and the state agency subsequently fails to provide the counseling, information, and referrals.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/8736","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hr/BILLSTATUS-119hr8736.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:29:51.198Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hr-8736","plainSummary":"This bill would make it easier for employers to pay workers with disabilities less than the minimum wage under special certificates. It removes a requirement that younger workers with disabilities receive transition services and vocational rehabilitation before starting subminimum-wage work. It also reduces employer obligations to connect these workers with career counseling and other services from state agencies.","industries":[{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Disability rights organizations and advocacy groups have a direct stake in policies governing subminimum wage employment for people with disabilities."},{"industry":"labor-unions","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"The bill affects wage standards and employment protections for workers with disabilities, a core labor-standards issue."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Some health and rehabilitation service providers employ individuals with disabilities under special wage certificates and would face changed regulatory obligations."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"eb7589cc7965496c169b1fa3","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:31:49.542Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would loosen requirements that allow entities with a special wage certificate to pay subminimum wages to people with disabilities.","whoItTouches":"People at work, and the people who employ them.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in your job, your hours and your pay. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-hr-8736","bioguideId":"B001301","cycle":2026,"score":0.020246375512604185,"matches":[{"industry":"labor-unions","donorAmount":47000,"donorShare":0.03382935517391052,"billConfidence":0.75,"contribution":0.012081912562110901},{"industry":"health-providers","donorAmount":34041.02,"donorShare":0.0245